       "marks": [
        "strong"
       ],
       "text": "History Of "
      },
      {
       "_key": "lr9paz3imt5v",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "strong",
        "9zspfco9eyha"
       ],
       "text": "Dakhla Oasis"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "9zspfco9eyha",
       "_type": "internalLink",
       "reference": {
        "_ref": "wp-page-58783",
        "_type": "reference"
       }
      }
     ],
     "style": "h2"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000003",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000002",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "The history of Dakhla Oasis, known as Zeszes in ancient times and later as El-Wah, is a tapestry of human settlement and civilization dating back to prehistoric times. This oasis, situated in Egypt’s Western Desert, has a rich and varied history that reveals much about the ancient and more recent past of the region."
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000005",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000004",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Prehistoric Settlements:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000008",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000006",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Early Inhabitants"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000007",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Evidence suggests that Dakhla was inhabited as early as 150,000 years ago. The discovery of fossils, tools, skeletons, and early hut foundations indicates a Neolithic presence, one of the earliest in Africa."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "00000000000e",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000009",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Neolithic Lifestyle"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000000a",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": The early inhabitants were pastoral, living near a large lake that once filled the areas of "
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000000c",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "00000000000b"
       ],
       "text": "Kharga"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000000d",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": " and Dakhla. Rock inscriptions depict a diverse wildlife including zebras, giraffes, elephants, among others."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000000b",
       "_pendingInternalRef": {
        "sourceLocale": "en",
        "wpUrl": "http://travel2egypt.org/kharga-oasis/"
       },
       "_type": "externalLink",
       "href": "/guide/kharga-oasis",
       "newTab": false
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000010",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000000f",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Ancient Egyptian Period:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000016",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000011",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Old and Middle Kingdom Significance"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000012",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Dakhla was an important settlement during these "
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000014",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "000000000013"
       ],
       "text": "periods"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000015",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ". Notable sites include a large settlement at Qila el-Daba, featuring administrative buildings, a palace, and a cemetery."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000013",
       "_type": "externalLink",
       "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Intermediate_Period_of_Egypt",
       "newTab": true
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000019",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000017",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Population Shifts"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000018",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": As the region became more arid, populations moved towards the more fertile Nile valley."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "00000000001b",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000001a",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Archaeological Expeditions:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "00000000001e",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000001c",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Ongoing Research"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000001d",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Teams of archaeologists, like the one led by Prof. Tony Mills, continue to uncover Dakhla’s history, aiming to establish a clearer picture of life in the desert during prehistoric times."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000020",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000001f",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Later Historical Developments:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000026",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000021",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "New Kingdom Changes"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000022",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": The capital of Dakhla moved to Mut, which remains the main city today. While much of its ancient glory has faded, intricate labyrinths and wooden doors from this "
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000024",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "000000000023"
       ],
       "text": "era"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000025",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": " still exist."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000023",
       "_type": "externalLink",
       "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_and_Roman_Egypt",
       "newTab": true
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000029",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000027",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Qasr Dakhla"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000028",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": An Islamic village established in medieval times, it is one of the oldest continuously inhabited villages in the oasis."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "00000000002b",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000002a",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Economic and Agricultural Importance:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000031",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000002c",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Taxation and Productivity"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000002d",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": In ancient times, Dakhla was known for its fertility, producing grains, wine, minerals, and fruit, leading to taxation by the "
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000002f",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "00000000002e"
       ],
       "text": "Pharaohs"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000030",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000002e",
       "_type": "externalLink",
       "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_pharaohs",
       "newTab": true
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000033",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000032",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Greek and Roman Periods:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000036",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000034",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Roman Occupation"
      },
      {
       "_key": "63u4ppihxz8z",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": While Greek presence is less evident, Roman occupation left significant marks, such as the Temple of Amun at "
      },
      {
       "_key": "or0a9dyc1egb",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "t3eyve47ljap"
       ],
       "text": "Deir el-Hagar"
      },
      {
       "_key": "sgevbq2jb8ct",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ". Several Roman sites are being unearthed, shedding light on the transition to Christian occupation."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "t3eyve47ljap",
       "_type": "internalLink",
       "reference": {
        "_ref": "guideArticle.dakhla-oasis.deir-el-hagar",
        "_type": "reference"
       }
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000038",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000037",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Christian and Arab Influence:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "00000000003b",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000039",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Byzantine and Christian Sites"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000003a",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Towns like Ismant el-Kharab and Kellis were significant Christian centers, as evidenced by early churches."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000041",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000003c",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Arab Invasion"
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000003d",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Prior to reaching the oases, Arabs invaded the area. Qasr Dakhla houses structures from the 12th century, signifying "
      },
      {
       "_key": "00000000003f",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [
        "00000000003e"
       ],
       "text": "the Ayyubid period"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000040",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [
      {
       "_key": "00000000003e",
       "_type": "externalLink",
       "href": "https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ayyubid-dynasty",
       "newTab": true
      }
     ],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000043",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000042",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Modern Exploration and Preservation:"
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "h3"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000046",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000044",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Current Efforts"
      },
      {
       "_key": "000000000045",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": ": Restoration and excavation efforts continue to reveal Dakhla’s rich history, providing insights into various historical periods, from Roman times through the Byzantine and Islamic eras."
      }
     ],
     "level": 1,
     "listItem": "bullet",
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
    {
     "_key": "000000000048",
     "_type": "block",
     "children": [
      {
       "_key": "000000000047",
       "_type": "span",
       "marks": [],
       "text": "Dakhla Oasis, with its deep historical roots and ongoing archaeological discoveries, remains a significant site for understanding the human past in Egypt’s Western Desert."
      }
     ],
     "markDefs": [],
     "style": "normal"
    },
